        "excerpt_selection.md": "# Excerpt Selection: TX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_2_v1\n\nGenerated: 2026-04-30\n\n## Selection Metadata\n- **Curriculum:** Bluebonnet (TX_BBO_XX)\n- **Grade:** G5\n- **Lesson ID:** TX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_2_v1\n- **Standard:** TEKS 5.7.C; TEKS 5.10.D\n- **Learning Goal:** Students will quote accurately from the text when identifying and describing personification.\n- **Reading Range (from planning doc):** All of Chapter 2\n\n## Excerpt Scope\n- **Selected Paragraphs:** All of Chapter 2 (paragraphs 1-11)\n- **Excerpt Structure:** Single passage\n- **Total Length:** ~665 words (~11 paragraphs)\n- **Scope Source:** Module plan directive (\"All of Chapter 2\")\n- **Confidence:** High (author explicitly specified full chapter)\n- **Path:** Extract (full chapter — no selection needed)\n\n## Numbering\n- **Curriculum behavior:** `sequential` (from excerpt_numbering_rules.md — TX_BBO is novel-based, no source numbering)\n- **Numbering authority:** Sequential count from (1)\n- **Teacher edition anchors found:** Yes — teacher lesson plan says \"Look at paragraph 7, the part of the story when the narrator steps off the train.\" Our paragraph 7 = \"I took one step off the train. The sticky moisture in the air kissed my face...\" — CONFIRMED match.\n- **Source numbering detected:** No — source text has no paragraph numbering. Page numbers (4, 5, 6) appear mid-paragraph and were removed during extraction.\n- **Convention applied:** sequential from (1)\n- **Activity range:** (1)-(11)\n- **Source range:** Full Chapter 2, paragraphs (1)-(11)\n- **Boundary notes:** Two paragraphs in the source .txt file were split by page numbers embedded in the text (\"4\" between paragraphs 4's halves, \"5\" between paragraph 10's halves). These were rejoined as single paragraphs based on continuity of content. The chapter title \"Chapter 2: My Journey to Houston\" and standalone page numbers (4, 5, 6) were excluded from paragraph counting.\n\n## Rationale\nThe module plan specifies \"All of Chapter 2\" as the text segment. The full chapter (665 words, 11 paragraphs) is an appropriate length for an elementary Close Read activity — it falls within the typical exemplar range (1-3 paragraph excerpts observed in HMH G5 exemplars, but this is a short chapter well-suited to complete use). The chapter contains three clear personification examples: paragraph 7 (\"The sticky moisture in the air kissed my face\" and \"The sun was stretching its warmth in every direction\") and paragraph 9 (\"It was calling my name\"), which provide sufficient material for the lesson's 5 driving questions + target task. No content was excluded.\n\n## Supplemental Guidance Applied\nPipeline-mode source used (curriculum_context.md). No Bluebonnet-specific excerpt length norms available — using universal defaults. Exemplar calibration from exemplars.json: selected exemplars use 1-3 paragraphs, but this is a short self-contained chapter and the module plan is explicit about \"All of Chapter 2.\"\n\n---\n\n## Numbering Manifest\n\n| # | First words | Word count |\n|---|------------|------------|\n| (1) | \"I was sitting down on a wooden bench at the train station, daydreaming…\" | 84 |\n| (2) | \"As I waited, I observed the people who were crowded on the station platform…\" | 66 |\n| (3) | \"I then began to wonder if I would come across any other Japanese people…\" | 71 |\n| (4) | \"My thoughts were interrupted by a porter requesting to see my ticket…window. I watched as everyone else boarded…\" | 66 |\n| (5) | \"Moments later, I felt the engines start, and soon the train rumbled…\" | 79 |\n| (6) | \"'Last stop, Erin Station!' alerted the conductor. The announcement startled me…\" | 42 |\n| (7) | \"I took one step off the train. The sticky moisture in the air kissed my face…\" | 53 |\n| (8) | \"I traveled all over the town. Everyone I encountered was cordial and welcoming…\" | 32 |\n| (9) | \"Eventually, I saw a large plot of flat land. It was calling my name…\" | 62 |\n| (10) | \"As I imagined the seeds growing in the Texas soil…to eventually export our crops globally. I felt self-assured…\" | 69 |\n| (11) | \"I departed this little town and started my journey back to Japan…\" | 41 |\n\n---\n\n## Extracted Text\n\n(1) I was sitting down on a wooden bench at the train station, daydreaming, when I heard the train wheels screech to a halt and the conductor demand, \"All aboard!\" Porters carefully placed step stools next to each vestibule and waited to assist passengers, specifically the women dressed in elaborate garments with fancy luggage. Everyone rushed to form lines at their proper train car, including myself. I was anxious to complete the last leg of my journey to a little town outside of Houston, Texas.\n\n(2) As I waited, I observed the people who were crowded on the station platform. Directly to my left, I saw a little girl around four years old, humming a jaunty tune. In the distance, I noticed a young couple holding hands. Behind me was a businessman carrying his brown leather briefcase, seemingly arriving at the station in a rush. None of the people, however, were Japanese.\n\n(3) I then began to wonder if I would come across any other Japanese people while in Houston. A gentleman at the World's Fair told me that, to his understanding, very few Japanese people lived in the Houston area. However, he recommended I explore a part of town established by Seito Saibrara, who was also a Japanese farmer. It was reassuring to think I might find a piece of home in Houston.\n\n(4) My thoughts were interrupted by a porter requesting to see my ticket. When I boarded the train to Erin Station in Houston, Texas, I had to shuffle sideways through the narrow aisle. I placed my small suitcase overhead on the metal rack and took my seat next to the foggy window. I watched as everyone else boarded and got settled into their seats for the journey.\n\n(5) Moments later, I felt the engines start, and soon the train rumbled under my feet. I watched as the station drifted slowly into the distance. The train picked up speed, and the town blurred past my eyes. I settled in to rest as I listened to the engine hum a steady tune. I would arrive at Erin Station at noon, ready to find the best land possible for my Japanese seed rice. I closed my eyes and drifted off.\n\n(6) \"Last stop, Erin Station!\" alerted the conductor. The announcement startled me awake, and I saw Erin Station approaching. My heart raced with excitement. The train finally came to a stop, and everyone started gathering their belongings. We were all anxious to disembark.\n\n(7) I took one step off the train. The sticky moisture in the air kissed my face. The sun was stretching its warmth in every direction. This climate will be perfect for cultivating rice, I thought to myself. I rushed from the station to search for the perfect area to sow my rice seeds.\n\n(8) I traveled all over the town. Everyone I encountered was cordial and welcoming. I had reservations that I would not be accepted here as a Japanese man, but my doubts quickly vanished.\n\n(9) Eventually, I saw a large plot of flat land. It was calling my name. I reached down and grabbed a handful of soil. The soil was moist in the palm of my hand. It was absolutely perfect. Houston, Texas, was indeed an exceptional place for Japanese rice cultivation. I closed my eyes and envisioned the bountiful rice crops growing on this acreage.\n\n(10) As I imagined the seeds growing in the Texas soil, I also began to contemplate how we would distribute our products. It dawned on me that the railways I just traveled on would be a perfect way to distribute our rice throughout the states, and the Houston seaports could help us to eventually export our crops globally. I felt self-assured that this plan of mine was going to work.\n\n(11) I departed this little town and started my journey back to Japan. I could not wait to convince some fellow Japanese men to partake in this venture with me! But first, I would have to share the news with my wife.\n",
